{"i": 0, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"lost‑wax sprue tree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Lost-Wax Sprue Tree\n\n'Twas sprue-morn, and the lost-wax gleamed,\nThe patterns did the furnace fear;\nAll crunched and burnt, the models seemed\nTo melt, and disappear.\n\n\"Beware the Sprue Tree, oh my son!\nThe branches coil, the castings fall!\nBeware the Mold-Beast, every one,\nAnd shun the Pouring-Hall!\"\n\nHe took his trusty tool in hand:\nLong time the sprue-ful foe he sought—\nSo rested he by Sand-Cast land,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in thought he stood, the Sprue,\nWith eyes of gleam and breath of spray,\nCame burbling through the furnace, too,\nAnd chiselled out its way!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe tool went snickle-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its crew\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Sprue Tree dread?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas sprue-morn, and the lost-wax gleamed,\nThe patterns did the furnace fear;\nAll crunched and burnt, the models seemed\nTo melt, and disappear.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 1, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"kintsugi seam of gold\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Kintsugi Gold\n\n'Twas brillig, and the mended pot\nDid gimbled with a golden seam;\nAll splinterous were the breaks it got,\nAnd filled with molten dream.\n\n\"Beware the cracks, the chips and craze!\nThe pottery that falls in bits!\nBeware the flaw that always faze,\nAnd make the ceram-flits!\"\n\nHe took his brush, a tiny quill;\nLong time the broken pieces sought.\nThen rested he by the window sill,\nAnd kintsugi he wrought.\n\nAnd as in thought he stood and eyed,\nThe Golden Seam, with shimmer bright,\nCame glinting through the breach it hied,\nAnd filled the pot with light.\n\nOh, glorious seam! Oh, gilded line!\nIts beauty did the soul enchant!\nHe chortled in his joy divine,\nA truly splendid plant!\n\n'Twas brillig, and the mended pot\nDid gimbled with a golden seam;\nAll splinterous were the breaks it got,\nAnd filled with molten dream.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 2, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"alpenglow on scree\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Alpenglow on Scree\n\n'Twas alpenglow, and the scree-slopes did gleam,\nThe cragsmen did grumble and grugle and gnoam;\nAll shimmy-shale were the grick-stones, I deem,\nAnd the slithy-slide rattled from home.\n\n\"Beware the Flumming-Fell! Oh, the tooth and the claw!\nBeware the Gloom-Grit, and the Wrimbled-Wind's woe!\"\nHe took up his truncheon, with shuddering awe,\nAnd sought by the scree-slopes, to and fro.\n\nAnd as in wild bluster, he shlumped in his might,\nThe Flumming-Fell, with eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the scree-drift, to his fright,\nAnd burbled its gruesome, grumbling name!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe truncheon went snicker-snick-snack!\nHe left it a-splutter, and whiffling anew,\nHe went grump-glug-glumming back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Flumming-Fell?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas alpenglow, and the scree-slopes did gleam,\nThe cragsmen did grumble and grugle and gnoam;\nAll shimmy-shale were the grick-stones, I deem,\nAnd the slithy-slide rattled from home.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 3, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"plane iron camber\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Camberwock\n\n'Twas brillig, and the Plane-irons did gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the Grindstones, and the mortice-gauges outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Camberwock, my son! The jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Bevel-grout, and shun the frumious Sharpening-snatch!\"\n\nHe took his trusty Jointer-plane; long time the Tool-box foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Trestle-tree, and stood a while in thought.\n\nAnd, as in thought he stood so deep, the Camberwock, with eyes aflame,\nCame whiffling through the Work-bench wood, and burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through the trusty Plane-iron went,\nThe blade did cleave, the shavings flew, and left it truly rent.\n\nAnd with its head he went galumphing back to where the sawdust slept;\nAnd said, \"O, perfect Plane, my lad! It's been so smooth!\" he wept.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the Plane-irons did gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the Grindstones, and the mortice-gauges outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 4, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"badger knot bloom\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Badgerknotted Bloom\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.\n\n\"Beware the Badgerknotted Bloom, my son!\nThe gnashing teeth, the grasping paw!\nBeware the Snickerdoodle, and shun\nThe frumious Bumble-gnaw!\"\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Badgerknotted Bloom, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffly through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Badgerknotted Bloom?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\nDid gyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll mimsy were the borogoves,\nAnd the mome raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 5, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"summit prayer flag\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Prayer-Flag's Plaint**\n\n'Twas high, and the summit was windy and stark,\nThe ice-creeched, and rime-flowers did cling;\nAll blurry the sun was, a wan, frosty mark,\nAnd the prayer-flag went flapping to sing.\n\nBehold the Prayer-Flag, with its tatteredy hues,\nThe sun-bleached, the wind-torn, the thin!\nIts mantra-scrawl faded, where the cold did infuse,\nAnd the blizzard-blasts whistled therein.\n\nIt fluttered, it shivered, it moaned on the pole,\n\"Beware the Greath-Silence, my child!\nFor its breath it does chill to the deeps of the soul,\nAnd leaves heart-prayers utterly wild!\"\n\nAnd as it thus flapped, with a whimpering wail,\nA Glacian-Mist rolled from the brink;\nAnd it swallowed the prayer-flag, both tattered and pale,\nAnd left not a jingle, I think.\n\n\"And hast thou then seen it, the Glacian-Mist grim?\"\nThe wanderer chuntered with fear;\n\"The prayer-flag it took, from the summit's high brim,\nAnd left us no solace to hear!\"\n\n'Twas high, and the summit was windy and stark,\nThe ice-creeched, and rime-flowers did cling;\nAll blurry the sun was, a wan, frosty mark,\nAnd the prayer-flag no longer did sing.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
