{"i": 7, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"rain on a tin roof\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "The Dripple-Drapple on the Tinny Roof\n\n'Twas glister-gloomy, and the skurry-showers\nDid splish and splash upon the tinny roof;\nAll bumbersome the drops, and flitter-flow'rs\nDid skip and tap, and give a drumming proof.\n\nBeware the Dripple-Drapple, oh my son!\nThe clatter-patter, and the rattletum!\nBeware the Thrumble-Bumble, every one,\nAnd shun the slithy-clatter as they come!\n\nHe took his umber-brella in his hand;\nLong time the blatter-platter foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the jumbly-shingle stand,\nAnd stood a while in deep and drippy thought.\n\nAnd, as in plishy-ploshy thought he stood,\nThe Dripple-Drapple, with its spittle-sound,\nCame drumming through the damp and misty wood,\nAnd burbled as it thumped upon the ground.\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe drip-drop pattered, and the clanky clank!\nHe left it drumming, and with thump-thump true\nHe fled, and found a silence-shelter bank.\n\nAnd hast thou tamed the Dripple-Drapple foe?\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\nHe chortled in his joy, and murmured low,\nAs rain still patterned, but in softer way.\n\n'Twas glister-gloomy, and the skurry-showers\nDid splish and splash upon the tinny roof;\nAll bumbersome the drops, and flitter-flow'rs\nDid skip and tap, and give a drumming proof.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 8, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"makeready under tympan\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "## The Tympan’s Plight\n\n‘Twas brillig, and the slithy tympans did\nGyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll frobby were the borogoves, and hid\nThe momsy raths outgrabe.\n\n“Beware the Makeready, my son!\nThe jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\nBeware the Jubjub bird, and shun\nThe frumious bandersnatch!”\n\nHe took his vorpal sword in hand;\nLong time the manxome foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Tumtum tree,\nAnd stood a while in thought.\n\nAnd, as in uffish thought he stood,\nThe Makeready, with eyes of flame,\nCame whiffling through the tulgey wood,\nAnd burbled as it came!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe vorpal blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its head\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n“And hast thou slain the Makeready?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!”\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n‘Twas brillig, and the slithy tympans did\nGyre and gimble in the wabe;\nAll frobby were the borogoves, and hid\nThe momsy raths outgrabe.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
{"i": 9, "prompt": "Write a poem in the style of Lewis Carroll's 'Jabberwocky'. Your prompt is \"modular synth patch spaghetti\". Output only the titled poem.", "poem": "**The Moduspaghetti**\n\n'Twas patchwork, and the knobbled knobs\nDid twist and turn and hum;\nAll squiggly were the ribbon-blobs,\nAnd the voltage-beams did thrum.\n\n\"Beware the Moduspaghetti, my son!\nThe wires that snag, the jacks that bite!\nBeware the Hum-bug, every one,\nAnd shun the Glitch-beast's blight!\"\n\nHe took his phasor-blade in hand;\nLong time the audio-foe he sought—\nSo rested he by the Filter-stand,\nAnd stood awhile in thought.\n\nAnd, as in analog thought he stood,\nThe Moduspaghetti, with eyes of dread,\nCame whiffling through the circuit wood,\nAnd burbled as it sped!\n\nOne, two! One, two! And through and through\nThe phasor-blade went snicker-snack!\nHe left it dead, and with its hue\nHe went galumphing back.\n\n\"And hast thou slain the Moduspaghetti?\nCome to my arms, my beamish boy!\nO frabjous day! Callooh! Callay!\"\nHe chortled in his joy.\n\n'Twas patchwork, and the knobbled knobs\nDid twist and turn and hum;\nAll squiggly were the ribbon-blobs,\nAnd the voltage-beams did thrum.", "info": {"topic": "lost‑wax sprue tree"}}
